    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Zack Gelof** (66.86) and **Brendan Donovan** (64.97) anchor the lineup as our most efficient plays. **Zack Gelof** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost against Cody Bradford\u2019s weak 5.29 xERA, compounded by an favorable park factor and a top-of-the-order assignment that maximizes his scoring potential. Similarly, **Brendan Donovan** is elevated by a massive 14% platoon advantage and a leadoff slot against the middling Hunter Brown. Meanwhile, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (62.55) and **Bryce Harper** (62.13) remain locked into the heart of the order, where their high floors and strong lineup protection provide the necessary stability to counter the occasional volatility of our mid-tier SP matchups.\n\nThe primary lineup optimization struggle today centered on the outfield, where **Justin Crawford** (51.68) narrowly missed the final slot. While **Justin Crawford** offers elite speed potential, the optimizer favored the higher efficiency and park-adjusted scoring of **Lawrence Butler** (62.28) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (56.22). **Lawrence Butler** is a high-upside play today because the combination of park factors and the favorable matchup against Cody Bradford pushes his raw efficiency well past his baseline. **Dylan Beavers** (57.08) also earns a start over the bench reserves largely due to his tactical advantage against the high-walk, slow-delivery profile of Freddy Peralta, which creates a prime window for **Dylan Beavers** to exploit the stolen base category, our most precious commodity.\n\nRisk management remains a theme as we navigate \"Lineup Pending\" designations across the board. While the projected scores for **Yainer Diaz** (40.78) and **Cal Raleigh** (55.77) are mathematically sound, both are currently tethered to bottom-third order positions, which drags down their ceiling. Managers should monitor official lineups closely; if **Yainer Diaz** drops out of the order or **Cal Raleigh** faces unexpected defensive substitution risks, our efficiency floor could collapse. Furthermore, despite the high score for **Daulton Varsho** (53.89), his matchup against the elite skill set of Bryan Woo (3.57 xERA) serves as a reminder to balance aggressive starts with the preservation of our limited game caps as we head into the late-season stretch.",
